WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.780
Hi, and welcome back to
another episode with ItsDrDan.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.300
And today we're going to be
looking specifically at chirality.

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:15.900
In this particular episode of
chirality, we're going to be learning

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:18.190
how to find the chiral carbons.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.919
So let's get into exactly
what we're looking at when

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:22.299
it comes to a chiral carbon.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:26.160
The whole thing with chirality is
that these are optical isomers.

00:00:26.169 --> 00:00:27.990
So they are stereoisomers.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.520
So it's all about how are they
positioned in three dimensional space.

00:00:32.129 --> 00:00:36.000
Versus a constitutional isomer, which
is just about how are you changing

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.910
where branches go or substituents?

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:41.739
So in order to do this, there's some
little quick little steps for us to be

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:43.310
able to try to remember how to do this.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:48.139
The first thing is that carbons
need to have four groups

00:00:48.179 --> 00:00:49.520
around them no matter what.

00:00:49.959 --> 00:00:54.490
So whenever we are looking at this,
it needs to have four unique groups.

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:58.710
So when we count four, right, we need
to then check are they all unique?

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:01.480
Meaning, they all have to be different.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:07.250
So a hydrogen is different than
a CH3 versus an OH or a one side

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:08.980
of the molecule versus this one.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:10.910
We need to make sure they are different.

00:01:11.009 --> 00:01:13.350
If they are, then this is chiral.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:17.470
Now sometimes you're going to also
have it where you can have four

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:19.399
things and some of them are identical.

00:01:19.869 --> 00:01:24.090
So we need to realize that chiral
is when you have four unique things.

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:30.330
And achiral, or not chiral, right, is when
you are ignoring groups that, whenever

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.830
you have three or less groups, so when
you're looking around that carbon, and

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:37.039
we're trying to count, like, all the
little bonds around it to three or less,

00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:43.320
so you want to ignore things like double
bonds, CH3s, CH2, When you are looking

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:47.460
at these structures now, this might seem
a little confusing at first, but let's

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.270
take a look at an example and go through
these steps and we'll go through several

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.010
practice prompts to understand this.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:54.360
Let's go, let's go look at it.

00:01:58.859 --> 00:02:04.900
Let's identify the chiral centers
or chiral carbons or stereo centers.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:08.190
There are several different words
that are used to determine this.

00:02:09.524 --> 00:02:12.225
If you remember, the first thing
that we had to do is carbons need

00:02:12.235 --> 00:02:14.265
to have four different groups.

00:02:14.575 --> 00:02:17.165
So let's first try to identify those.

00:02:17.635 --> 00:02:20.984
So if I'm looking at the following
structure, we can try to find the

00:02:20.985 --> 00:02:22.724
carbons with four different groups.

00:02:22.735 --> 00:02:27.034
So if I look at the CH3, for
example, it has three hydrogens

00:02:27.374 --> 00:02:30.605
and then also one CH bond, right?

00:02:30.605 --> 00:02:31.685
So that has four things.

00:02:32.204 --> 00:02:35.575
Now, keep in mind though, it
needs to have four unique groups.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:39.410
So what we're going to be doing
here is going from carbon to carbon

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.080
and looking at every single group.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:44.649
So a CH3, does that
have four unique groups?

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.670
Well, three of them are
hydrogen, so the answer is no.

00:02:48.019 --> 00:02:50.600
So that is not going
to be a chiral center.

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:54.120
So let's now move to our next carbon.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.990
So let's take a look right here.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:59.420
So if I look at this one,
what do we exactly do we see?

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.650
We got to count every single group.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.040
So when, what do I mean when I say
that, right, is that we're counting This

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:12.140
chlorine here as a group, this hydrogen
here as another group, the CH3, and

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:17.669
then we are counting this entire side
of the molecule as the other group.

00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:21.109
So we got four completely
different things, right?

00:03:21.109 --> 00:03:26.820
You have a chlorine, an H, a
CH3, so all of those are four

00:03:26.829 --> 00:03:29.120
completely different groups.

00:03:29.725 --> 00:03:31.855
I'm just going to put
big part molecule here.

00:03:32.235 --> 00:03:34.185
So we have all those four groups.

00:03:34.195 --> 00:03:38.735
So what does that tell you is that
this particular carbon, this is chiral.

00:03:38.765 --> 00:03:42.754
So generally with these classes, you put
a little star next to it to tell you, Hey,

00:03:42.765 --> 00:03:45.685
when if this has a mirror image to it.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:50.220
There's going to be another form of it
that exists because bonds can potentially

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:54.980
flip and change Being that they are
commonly shown as wedges and dashes.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:00.510
So let's take a look at other carbons
in this molecule Is that the only one?

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:02.700
Well, let's go to the next one.

00:04:03.199 --> 00:04:08.000
So if I go down to this carbon here Well
same general idea you want to count are

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.010
there four completely different groups?

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:16.755
So we have a hydrogen We have an OH, we
have the C double bond O, OH, and then we

00:04:16.765 --> 00:04:19.555
have the entire top part of the molecule.

00:04:20.085 --> 00:04:22.535
This is very similar to geometries, right?

00:04:22.535 --> 00:04:25.755
You're trying to see, like, is
it a tetrahedral geometry, right?

00:04:26.075 --> 00:04:29.054
So four groups, are they
all completely unique?

00:04:29.084 --> 00:04:29.924
Which they are.

00:04:30.235 --> 00:04:35.065
So what this tells you is that, hey,
once again, we got another chiral center.

00:04:35.725 --> 00:04:37.395
Is this all the ones that we have?

00:04:37.395 --> 00:04:38.765
Well, let's try to take a look.

00:04:39.245 --> 00:04:42.635
So we have one more carbon we can
look at right over here on the end.

00:04:43.095 --> 00:04:44.755
Now, is this one that we can count?

00:04:44.785 --> 00:04:46.225
Well, how many groups do we have?

00:04:46.225 --> 00:04:50.605
Well, we see we have one, two,
three groups on all sides.

00:04:51.004 --> 00:04:52.534
So this is one that's achiral.

00:04:53.104 --> 00:04:54.024
It's not achiral carbon.

00:04:56.475 --> 00:05:01.095
When it comes to trying to label
molecules now, so whenever you have

00:05:01.604 --> 00:05:06.735
a chiral carbon, that means the
entire molecule is also chiral.

00:05:07.055 --> 00:05:14.744
So our molecule here is a chiral
molecule, and it has two chiral

00:05:14.755 --> 00:05:17.235
centers, or chiral carbons.

00:05:17.884 --> 00:05:21.614
Those terms can be used generally,
they can be exchanged, they

00:05:21.614 --> 00:05:22.664
mean the exact same thing.

00:05:22.684 --> 00:05:24.804
So two chiral centers, or carbons.

00:05:26.624 --> 00:05:26.905
All right.

00:05:26.924 --> 00:05:28.695
And they both start to show that.

00:05:29.195 --> 00:05:33.594
Let's go through some several different
other examples to kind of illustrate this.

00:05:33.954 --> 00:05:36.854
And we'll see if we can
keep, keep practicing this.

00:05:36.854 --> 00:05:39.195
And this is something where I
go through these next examples.

00:05:39.595 --> 00:05:42.774
Feel free to try to pause the video
and see, can you do it yourself?

00:05:42.955 --> 00:05:45.975
Because that's the ultimate way
to understanding is by practicing

00:05:45.975 --> 00:05:47.685
it as much as you possibly can.

00:05:48.234 --> 00:05:50.445
Let's go on to our next few examples.

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.520
All right, let's take a look at
all these different examples that

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.500
we have where we are trying to
identify all of the chiral carbons.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:59.530
So, what are we going to do?

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:02.890
Let's go from molecule to molecule,
and what we're going to see is,

00:06:03.109 --> 00:06:05.760
can we go from carbon to carbon
and see if they are chiral?

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.999
Right here we have three
different carbons, so we have

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.560
one, two, three across the board.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:15.000
So let's look at the first one.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:18.420
Does it have four groups,
and are they all unique?

00:06:18.785 --> 00:06:21.905
So if a lot of times these are
drawn as geometric or skeletal

00:06:21.905 --> 00:06:25.925
structures, so we have to remember
that there are hidden hydrogens on

00:06:25.925 --> 00:06:27.375
every single one of these molecules.

00:06:27.825 --> 00:06:29.235
So let's put them in.

00:06:29.534 --> 00:06:33.865
So if I kind of put on each one of
these carbons, I put a hydrogen in here.

00:06:33.865 --> 00:06:35.715
So we see we have two hydrogens here.

00:06:36.394 --> 00:06:40.395
We'll put our hydrogen on carbon
number two, and let's put our

00:06:40.395 --> 00:06:43.205
hydrogens on number three as well.

00:06:43.815 --> 00:06:48.215
So, once we do that, do we have four
unique groups on each of our carbons?

00:06:48.775 --> 00:06:49.855
Let's look at number one.

00:06:50.375 --> 00:06:53.735
So on number one, you can see
that you have two hydrogens

00:06:53.784 --> 00:06:55.455
on this particular molecule.

00:06:55.465 --> 00:06:59.104
So what that means is, it does
not have four unique carbons.

00:06:59.325 --> 00:07:01.515
So we can toss out carbon number one.

00:07:02.274 --> 00:07:06.045
If we move to number two, well, in
this case, well, let's take a look.

00:07:06.045 --> 00:07:12.685
We have One group on the left, one
group up top, the hydrogen counts

00:07:12.685 --> 00:07:16.185
as a group, and then you have
this entire group on the right.

00:07:16.785 --> 00:07:19.574
So, now is this one a chiral carbon?

00:07:19.995 --> 00:07:24.305
Well, this one is really tricky,
because yeah, it does have four

00:07:24.314 --> 00:07:26.784
groups, and they look pretty different.

00:07:27.314 --> 00:07:31.505
However, when it comes to a lot
of these, what we need to do is,

00:07:31.544 --> 00:07:34.854
imagine you draw like a little
imaginary line down the middle.

00:07:35.515 --> 00:07:40.055
And when I do that, notice how the
left hand and the right side, they're

00:07:40.085 --> 00:07:42.225
identical to one another, right?

00:07:42.305 --> 00:07:47.355
You have a CH2OH on the right
side, CH2OH on the left side.

00:07:47.884 --> 00:07:51.105
So what that means is two of
these groups are identical.

00:07:51.554 --> 00:07:53.204
So this one is not.

00:07:54.095 --> 00:07:58.365
Actually, as a result, it's still
a chiral on this particular carbon.

00:07:58.974 --> 00:08:05.635
So whenever something like this can
happen, chirality can be lost as a result.

00:08:06.184 --> 00:08:10.424
So in this particular one, it isn't,
that carbon isn't chiral either.

00:08:10.845 --> 00:08:14.544
So we actually, and being that
carbon number three is the same.

00:08:15.115 --> 00:08:20.695
This one is an achiral molecule, so
that means that we don't have any chiral

00:08:20.695 --> 00:08:25.105
centers, and that, that's okay, most
molecules they don't, so we always have to

00:08:25.105 --> 00:08:27.055
kind of check, go from carbon to carbon.

00:08:27.635 --> 00:08:28.935
So let's do the same thing here.

00:08:29.604 --> 00:08:34.375
So if I do the first one, right, so number
one here, we see that we have three CH3,

00:08:34.505 --> 00:08:36.365
we have three hydrogens, right, on CH3.

00:08:37.164 --> 00:08:39.525
So that tells you, no, we're
going to immediately toss that

00:08:39.525 --> 00:08:42.015
out, it is not for unique groups.

00:08:42.814 --> 00:08:46.205
If I look on carbon two, well, okay,
this one might be a possibility

00:08:46.425 --> 00:08:50.305
because one of the things that we're
looking for is do you see four bonds?

00:08:50.305 --> 00:08:52.255
Does it have that tetrahedral geometry?

00:08:52.615 --> 00:08:53.555
Are they all unique?

00:08:53.805 --> 00:09:00.520
So let's see, we have a CH3, we have
the hydrogen that's here, the OH, And

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:03.170
then the C double bond O CH3 side.

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:07.190
So yes, we do, we have four
completely unique groups here.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:11.339
So we can star that carbon for
ourselves and then determine, yes, we

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.399
actually do have a chiral carbon here.

00:09:14.059 --> 00:09:17.370
So that's kind of the trick, that's
exactly what we want to do as

00:09:17.379 --> 00:09:18.630
we're going through this molecule.

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:21.690
And the more you practice this, the
more you're going to be able to see,

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.695
hey, These are really quick to find.

00:09:24.215 --> 00:09:27.725
Now if I look at carbon number three,
we only have three different groups.

00:09:28.015 --> 00:09:30.355
So just to circle that for
everybody out there, right?

00:09:30.355 --> 00:09:33.395
We have one, two, three groups, right?

00:09:33.395 --> 00:09:36.234
We're only referring to
groups, not number of bonds.

00:09:36.244 --> 00:09:39.545
It's just how many different
areas of electrons do we have?

00:09:39.944 --> 00:09:42.105
So when we have three,
it cannot be chiral.

00:09:42.285 --> 00:09:46.915
It needs to have that tetrahedral
geometry so we can toss that out.

00:09:47.375 --> 00:09:48.655
And now what does this mean?

00:09:48.665 --> 00:09:50.605
We have a chiral molecule.

00:09:51.344 --> 00:09:53.645
with one chiral carbon.

00:09:55.064 --> 00:09:56.435
Alright, just like that.

00:09:56.435 --> 00:10:00.495
We will write that down for ourselves
that we do have this chiral structure.

00:10:00.974 --> 00:10:01.995
Let's go on to the next.

00:10:02.385 --> 00:10:04.875
So ring structures are a little different.

00:10:05.244 --> 00:10:09.874
The big thing about these is that
you need to try to get an eye for

00:10:09.874 --> 00:10:11.535
finding different chiral groups.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:16.220
The things, the shapes that you want
to generally look for are whenever

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:20.760
you see the carbon structure have
that like Y shape, because it

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:22.589
means it does have four groups.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:26.050
The fourth one is always
hidden by a hydrogen, right?

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:31.140
Whenever we do a geometric group,
we assume that we have hydrogens.

00:10:31.444 --> 00:10:32.665
And we can show that here, right?

00:10:32.665 --> 00:10:33.745
We can fill in our structure.

00:10:33.745 --> 00:10:35.714
We can see, hey, this has a hydrogen here.

00:10:36.135 --> 00:10:37.475
This is a CH3.

00:10:37.915 --> 00:10:40.435
Uh, we can fill in all
those things for yourself.

00:10:40.695 --> 00:10:44.975
And it's really good practice, especially
if you're new to organic chemistry, is

00:10:44.994 --> 00:10:50.615
counting all of your chiral, not only
your chiral, but all of your hydrogens

00:10:50.655 --> 00:10:52.045
as you're going through these structures.

00:10:52.525 --> 00:10:53.835
So we'll fill these in.

00:10:54.065 --> 00:10:55.625
We'll put all the hydrogens here.

00:10:56.115 --> 00:11:01.675
And this is something that gives us an
idea of, okay, well, what kind of Where,

00:11:01.675 --> 00:11:04.864
where are my chiral carbons because we're
filling in all those missing pieces.

00:11:05.785 --> 00:11:08.064
So let's take a look at
our possible candidates.

00:11:08.435 --> 00:11:12.724
Now remember, if they have two
or more hydrogens, toss them out.

00:11:13.144 --> 00:11:15.944
If it's a double bond,
also we can toss it out.

00:11:16.435 --> 00:11:21.015
So the only two that are possible
are this one here on the bottom

00:11:21.125 --> 00:11:22.375
and the one with the chlorine.

00:11:22.815 --> 00:11:24.725
Now let's see, are they chiral?

00:11:24.735 --> 00:11:25.995
Let's verify.

00:11:26.555 --> 00:11:29.375
So if we do the bottom one,
what we can see is that we

00:11:29.375 --> 00:11:32.115
have a CH3, we have a hydrogen.

00:11:32.175 --> 00:11:37.035
Now remember from before, we gotta
check both sides of the molecule.

00:11:37.404 --> 00:11:40.275
So how do exactly do I do that?

00:11:40.285 --> 00:11:44.425
Well, when it comes to this kind
of structure, we want to once

00:11:44.425 --> 00:11:48.145
again try to draw an imaginary
line right down the middle.

00:11:48.699 --> 00:11:53.589
And what we're trying to see is, are both
sides of the molecule, are they the same?

00:11:53.899 --> 00:11:56.379
It's kind of like
comparing heads and tails.

00:11:56.509 --> 00:11:58.219
Are they the same on the coin?

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:02.849
So when I do that, right, I have
one side here on the left, and

00:12:02.849 --> 00:12:06.769
then let's use another color, and I
have a different side on the right.

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:08.400
Now, are they the same?

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:11.649
Well, on the right hand side,
we see that we have a chlorine.

00:12:12.024 --> 00:12:15.635
On the left hand side we have a
double bond, so that tells you, yeah,

00:12:15.665 --> 00:12:17.055
both of them are very different.

00:12:17.055 --> 00:12:21.194
It's not like the example, the
first example, where, oh, they are

00:12:21.194 --> 00:12:22.595
identical, these are different.

00:12:22.814 --> 00:12:23.255
So this is a chiral carbon.

00:12:23.255 --> 00:12:25.545
This can stay.

00:12:26.074 --> 00:12:30.594
Now, we can do the exact same thing
for the chlorine structure from before.

00:12:31.055 --> 00:12:33.205
Where hey, let's let's take a look at it.

00:12:33.205 --> 00:12:35.425
Let's uh, try to circle all the groups.

00:12:35.425 --> 00:12:39.935
So we have a chlorine We have a hydrogen
We're gonna draw a little line right

00:12:39.935 --> 00:12:44.875
down the middle and what we see is
Oh The top half has a double bond

00:12:45.155 --> 00:12:50.055
and the bottom half does not so that
star also can stay in the structure

00:12:50.395 --> 00:12:52.565
That this is another chiral carbon.

00:12:52.575 --> 00:12:56.475
So we this is chiral
with two chiral carbons.

00:12:56.585 --> 00:12:56.825
Okay.

00:12:56.825 --> 00:12:57.915
So let's write that down.

00:12:58.375 --> 00:13:01.875
Chiral with two chiral carbons.

00:13:03.285 --> 00:13:06.365
All right, let's take a look
at our last example here.

00:13:06.605 --> 00:13:08.245
O' sorry, our second to last example.

00:13:08.784 --> 00:13:09.535
Now, what about this one?

00:13:10.045 --> 00:13:11.115
Well, same idea, right?

00:13:11.115 --> 00:13:14.595
We want to look for that triangle shape,
try to look for three and four groups.

00:13:14.944 --> 00:13:17.805
So the first one that comes to
mind is this one right here.

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:21.859
Now, being that it's the only one, all
the rest have two hydrogens, right?

00:13:21.859 --> 00:13:26.400
So every single one of these have
two hydrogens on each of them.

00:13:26.729 --> 00:13:31.359
So we don't have to worry about trying to
consider whether or not they're chiral.

00:13:31.709 --> 00:13:35.250
But this one, on the other
hand, it only has one.

00:13:35.269 --> 00:13:36.300
So this is possible.

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:41.209
Now, what about the two groups
on both sides of the coin?

00:13:41.650 --> 00:13:45.509
Well, if I draw a little line
here, well, what do we see?

00:13:45.509 --> 00:13:50.319
We have one side over here and
then another side on the right.

00:13:50.919 --> 00:13:51.760
Are they the same?

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:55.740
Well, we have CH2CH2 on both
sides and they meet in the middle.

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:56.969
They're identical.

00:13:56.970 --> 00:14:00.449
So what that tells you is that
